1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Lighthouses cut the sky with a shaft of light to guide distant wanderers to safety.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Technological and engineering masterpieces, their greatest achievement may be their haunting beauty.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Now, Lighthouses on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
At the junction of opposing forces,

5
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:48,600
land and sea,

6
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:51,460
light and dark,

7
00:00:54,460 --> 00:00:56,440
safety and peril,

8
00:00:56,441 --> 00:01:00,800
calm and chaos

9
00:01:00,801 --> 00:01:05,620
sits a lighthouse

10
00:01:05,621 --> 00:01:12,480
perched solidly on the very precipice of ruin

11
00:01:12,481 --> 00:01:17,320
lighthouses offer all a guidepost to safe passage

12
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:22,740
sending out a beacon of light

13
00:01:22,741 --> 00:01:25,500
whose fundamental symbolism is so evocative

14
00:01:25,501 --> 00:01:28,980
it sometimes overshadows the structures themselves.

15
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:35,980
Despite ever-advancing technology that threatens to make them obsolete,

16
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:39,720
lighthouses continue to serve as aids to navigation.

17
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:44,340
Roughly 800 still stand in the United States alone.

18
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:51,260
Their resiliency is testimony to the marvel of their construction and the power of their appeal.

19
00:01:51,261 --> 00:01:57,000
I think of it as like a primal resonance of the image of a light flashing in the darkness,

20
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:02,260
that guiding light in the darkness or in a storm that is only there for one purpose.

21
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:08,220
They're the antithesis of the city, the city with the sirens and the jackhammers going off

22
00:02:08,221 --> 00:02:09,940
and all of the noise and the stress.

23
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:16,400
The lighthouse is out there in a quiet area with a lap of wave and the caw of the gullet.

24
00:02:16,401 --> 00:02:23,400
Lighthouses flash their beacons from the four corners of the United States, plus Alaska and Hawaii.

25
00:02:23,401 --> 00:02:32,400
They blink along the entire coast of the Great Lakes.

26
00:02:32,401 --> 00:02:37,400
They stand firm amidst roiling surf.

27
00:02:37,401 --> 00:02:42,400
They have even floated as light ships over the most dangerous shoals.

28
00:02:42,401 --> 00:02:49,400
They keep sentinel anywhere earth, air and water conspire to create havoc.

29
00:02:49,401 --> 00:03:00,400
The meaning of lighthouses is found in the sacred trust that existed between the lighthouse keeper and those who traveled on the water.

30
00:03:00,401 --> 00:03:06,400
They serve the purpose of telling you where you are in the world.

31
00:03:06,401 --> 00:03:10,400
It's a purpose they have served since ancient times.

32
00:03:10,401 --> 00:03:17,400
Lighthouses have probably existed in some form or another since humans began negotiating the waterways.

33
00:03:17,401 --> 00:03:25,400
We can speculate that probably some of the first lighthouses or aids to navigation were fires on a cliff, a headland, or a beach,

34
00:03:25,401 --> 00:03:33,400
showing very early man the way back to safe harbor after having been out fishing or perhaps doing some sort of trade with neighbors.

35
00:03:33,401 --> 00:03:42,400
The first mention of lighthouses actually is as early as Homer talking about pillars of fire lighting the way into the port of Athens.

36
00:03:42,401 --> 00:03:47,400
One of the greatest lighthouses was also one of the earliest.

37
00:03:47,401 --> 00:03:53,400
The pharaohs of Alexandria in Egypt was completed by the Romans around 280 BC.

38
00:03:53,401 --> 00:04:04,400
It may have soared 450 feet with a massive square base, octagonal middle section, and a cylindrical tower.

39
00:04:04,401 --> 00:04:11,400
A ramp in the center probably enabled animals or slaves to haul fuel to the top of the tower.

40
00:04:11,401 --> 00:04:17,400
But in this dangerous time, the lighthouse was also a defense.

41
00:04:17,401 --> 00:04:26,860
As the state of Egypt was attacked by various invading armies, it was damaged, badly damaged.

42
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:34,080
It was damaged by earthquakes over the years, and eventually, in 1340, was completely felled.

43
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:37,120
What was left was completely felled by a massive earthquake.

44
00:04:38,280 --> 00:04:42,520
The Pharos of Alexandria is hailed as one of the seven wonders of the ancient world.

45
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:46,020
But it may not be the only lighthouse so recognized.

46
00:04:46,021 --> 00:04:52,520
There's another one of the seven ancient wonders of the world, the Colossus at Rhodes,

47
00:04:52,521 --> 00:04:58,120
which was a metal statue, a couple hundred feet high, and there is speculation that they

48
00:04:58,121 --> 00:05:05,120
either had a burning fire in the eyes or that this statue held a cup in an upraised hand

49
00:05:05,121 --> 00:05:07,720
which had a burning fire in it.

50
00:05:07,721 --> 00:05:13,620
The Romans also built more modest lighthouses in the far flung reaches of their empire.

51
00:05:13,621 --> 00:05:18,620
One survives in ruins at Dover Castle in England.

52
00:05:18,621 --> 00:05:22,980
These days, the ancient lighthouse at Dover has been joined by a couple of modern upstarts

53
00:05:22,981 --> 00:05:25,980
to guide ships, including hovercraft.

54
00:05:25,981 --> 00:05:34,720
Over a thousand years after the construction of the ancient Dover lighthouse, the dangerous

55
00:05:34,721 --> 00:05:39,120
Eddystone Reef off the coast of Plymouth, England, would witness a great technological

56
00:05:39,121 --> 00:05:40,940
leap forward for lighthouses.

57
00:05:40,941 --> 00:05:45,340
For the first time, a lighthouse would be built essentially in water.

58
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:50,655
There was a man by the name of Henry Winstanley, who was sort of a P.T.

59
00:05:50,656 --> 00:05:51,840
Barnum of his day.

60
00:05:52,280 --> 00:05:59,160
Winstanley was not an architect, and he wasn't an engineer, but he fashioned a lighthouse upon the Eddystone.

61
00:05:59,161 --> 00:06:01,920
A very difficult task. This is 1696.

62
00:06:01,921 --> 00:06:05,600
Everything is taken out there by sailing vessel.

63
00:06:05,601 --> 00:06:14,200
Out upon the slippery, wet rock with nothing but hand tools, Henry Winstanley set about building an 80-foot-high lighthouse.

64
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:21,620
Although his lighthouse managed to survive the winter, Winstanley tore it down and built a stronger one that spring.

65
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:25,980
A lighthouse that was 40 feet in diameter and 120 feet high.

66
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:28,980
And this one lasted until 1704.

67
00:06:28,981 --> 00:06:36,640
In that year, Henry and Workman and a relief keeper were in Plymouth and a storm struck.

68
00:06:37,840 --> 00:06:40,000
Winstanley and his men were in the lighthouse.

69
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:45,520
By the next morning, lighthouse and men, including Winstanley, were gone.

70
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:53,380
The next day, the night of the lighthouse was out of smooth wood, curved to deflect the crash of waves.

71
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:57,440
But one element of Winstanley's lighthouse would be revived.

72
00:06:57,441 --> 00:07:02,440
Both of these lighthouses were the first to have an enclosed lantern room.

73
00:07:02,441 --> 00:07:09,440
Henry Winstanley had 61-pound candles as an illuminant in his lantern room.

74
00:07:09,441 --> 00:07:15,440
Glass in the lantern room protected the light without hiding it, so it could be seen in all weather.

75
00:07:15,441 --> 00:07:22,440
Cleaner and more efficient fuels, such as candles and oil, replaced wood and tar as illuminants.

76
00:07:22,441 --> 00:07:23,846
as a luminance.

77
00:07:23,847 --> 00:07:31,440
In 1754, the latest Eddystone Lighthouse burned down and authorities commissioned

78
00:07:31,441 --> 00:07:32,791
a new structure.

79
00:07:32,792 --> 00:07:39,040
John Smedon was an engineer and an architect, and he constructed the first

80
00:07:39,041 --> 00:07:46,760
wave-swept lighthouse in the world out of granite. The Smedon Lighthouse tower has a

81
00:07:46,761 --> 00:07:51,100
taper to it, a very nice taper starting wide at the base, of course, and then working its

82
00:07:51,101 --> 00:07:55,617
way up and then just under the lantern room it flares out.

83
00:07:55,618 --> 00:07:57,720
And so any water that would

84
00:07:57,721 --> 00:08:04,600
run up the side would be forced away from the lantern room or the lighthouse.

85
00:08:04,601 --> 00:08:09,448
Because of climate changes, the Eddystone rocks were now submerged under the surf.

86
00:08:09,449 --> 00:08:10,149
Smeaton's

87
00:08:10,150 --> 00:08:12,811
lighthouse set essentially in open seas.

88
00:08:12,812 --> 00:08:16,280
The lighthouse was made up of giant jigsaw blocks

89
00:08:16,281 --> 00:08:20,960
which held together like gripping hands, using the force of waves to compress the structure.

90
00:08:21,580 --> 00:08:24,020
This dovetailed construction was very strong.

91
00:08:25,660 --> 00:08:32,600
In this manner, when the seas hit this structure, it actually causes this monolith to be stronger.

92
00:08:33,420 --> 00:08:39,760
Open sea lighthouses built into rocks were a marvel of construction that eventually found their way across the Atlantic.

93
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:45,960
Up next, America's most famous open sea lighthouse, Minot's Ledge Lighthouse.

94
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:51,300
Perched for nearly 150 years atop one of America's most dangerous rocks.

95
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:57,840
Michigan has over 100 lighthouses, more than any other state in the Union.

96
00:08:58,680 --> 00:09:01,520
Lighthouses will return on Modern Marvels.

97
00:09:03,560 --> 00:09:08,320
Even before John Smedon's remarkable lighthouse rose from the rocks of Plymouth, England,

98
00:09:09,180 --> 00:09:11,920
fires, most likely lanterns hoisted on sticks,

99
00:09:11,921 --> 00:09:14,780
were burning along the shores of Plymouth, Massachusetts,

100
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:16,960
and other villages in the New World.

101
00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:22,940
In 1716, America's first official lighthouse was built,

102
00:09:23,580 --> 00:09:27,000
and Boston Light still marks the entrance to Boston Harbor

103
00:09:27,001 --> 00:09:28,380
at Little Brewster Island.

104
00:09:31,440 --> 00:09:35,100
The original lighthouse was destroyed during the Revolutionary War,

105
00:09:35,540 --> 00:09:37,540
but was rebuilt in 1783.

106
00:09:37,541 --> 00:09:45,760
The 75-foot rubble stone tower was raised to its current height of 89 feet in 1859.

107
00:09:46,580 --> 00:09:51,260
The second auto-frenel lens was put up there in 1859.

108
00:09:52,160 --> 00:09:56,400
And electricity didn't come out to the island until 1948 after World War II.

109
00:09:57,460 --> 00:10:02,280
Boston needed a lighthouse because of the treacherous seas and high volume of trade.

110
00:10:02,281 --> 00:10:12,060
A lot of people don't realize that Boston was the hub for the colonies for a lot of years, up until the mid-1800s.

111
00:10:14,160 --> 00:10:16,540
Most of the shipping traffic came through Boston.

112
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:23,200
In my belief, that if they didn't put this lighthouse here and make Boston Harbor safe for ships to come in and out of,

113
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:28,380
it wouldn't have grown to be, as they called it back then, the hub of the universe.

114
00:10:28,381 --> 00:10:33,240
Rick Himmelrick is the lighthouse keeper at Little Brewster Island.

115
00:10:33,241 --> 00:10:37,620
He shares his duty with two other Coast Guard officers who rotate ships.

116
00:10:37,621 --> 00:10:43,740
Well, you know, I got the light to take care of.

117
00:10:43,741 --> 00:10:46,780
I've got the buildings and the grounds.

118
00:10:46,781 --> 00:10:51,660
And this time of year, most of my energies go into taking care of the buildings and grounds

119
00:10:51,661 --> 00:10:54,440
and making the place presentable for tourists.

120
00:10:54,441 --> 00:10:58,300
and, of course, taking care of the lens and the light itself.

121
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:03,380
That demands about an hour or better every day of my time.

122
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,800
About a four-hour stint once during the week

123
00:11:06,801 --> 00:11:09,080
where I've got to completely oil it, clean it, and everything.

124
00:11:09,081 --> 00:11:10,080
Thank you.

125
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:25,860
Their tasks do not differ markedly from keepers of the past.

126
00:11:26,360 --> 00:11:31,920
Though they no longer carry fuel up the tower, nor manually ring a fog bell, or fire fog cannons.

127
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:34,780
Nor are they isolated for months at a time.

128
00:11:35,480 --> 00:11:38,960
Though the weather still gets pretty nasty on Little Brewster Island,

129
00:11:39,080 --> 00:11:43,260
Northeast is, I mean, it gets kicking.

130
00:11:43,380 --> 00:11:46,920
You'll have 20-foot waves right out off the east end of the island here.

131
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:50,440
We'll have waves breaking against the back of the house and stuff all night long.

132
00:11:52,280 --> 00:11:55,300
I feel totally blessed, really.

133
00:11:55,420 --> 00:11:57,900
It's the only way I can describe it, to be entrusted with this.

134
00:11:59,180 --> 00:12:00,500
That's the way I approach it.

135
00:12:00,501 --> 00:12:06,000
The government, the public has entrusted me to take care of a national treasure.

136
00:12:09,080 --> 00:12:19,820
Within sight of the Boston station is Minot's Ledge near Cohasset, Massachusetts, a direct

137
00:12:19,821 --> 00:12:25,960
descendant of John Smeaton's dovetailed lighthouse in England.

138
00:12:25,961 --> 00:12:30,640
Too many wrecks off the coast prompted construction of a lighthouse here that is embedded into

139
00:12:30,641 --> 00:12:36,140
the rocky shoal roughly ten feet below high tide, in the middle of some of the roughest

140
00:12:36,141 --> 00:12:40,660
seas along any shore.

141
00:12:40,661 --> 00:12:42,920
And they realized they had to build something out there.

142
00:12:42,921 --> 00:12:48,700
So the first attempt was a pile structure, cast iron piles, there were nine of them,

143
00:12:48,701 --> 00:12:53,140
a center and eight around the perimeter, that were let into this Minot's ledge.

144
00:12:53,141 --> 00:12:58,220
Now this ledge is below water, at high water, so they had to work at low water.

145
00:12:58,221 --> 00:13:05,260
And they had to drill in the 1849, 1850 era, they had to drill by hand, auger into this

146
00:13:05,261 --> 00:13:12,020
ledge to put these piles, which were tall legs, and then a cottage-type structure on top.

147
00:13:12,021 --> 00:13:16,440
They thought that it would work because they thought the waves would just sweep right through

148
00:13:16,441 --> 00:13:20,509
that structure and not crash against it and do any serious damage to it.

149
00:13:20,510 --> 00:13:21,300
But of course,

150
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:27,980
unfortunately, they were proven wrong. In April 1851, a nor'easter blew in while

151
00:13:27,981 --> 00:13:31,134
two lightkeeper's assistants were manning the lighthouse.

152
00:13:31,135 --> 00:13:32,960
The people of Cohasset could hear

153
00:13:32,961 --> 00:13:34,404
the bell ringing out there.

154
00:13:34,405 --> 00:13:37,720
The next morning when they looked out, there was no more Minus

155
00:13:37,721 --> 00:13:44,248
Ledge. It had been swept right off the ledge. Both assistants died in the disaster.

156
00:13:44,249 --> 00:13:44,949
Still,

157
00:13:45,100 --> 00:13:49,580
a lighthouse was needed, and construction of a new lighthouse began under the direction

158
00:13:49,581 --> 00:13:54,144
of B.S. Alexander of the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers.

159
00:13:54,145 --> 00:13:56,680
A monolithic granite tower was

160
00:13:56,681 --> 00:14:02,100
planned combining some of Smeaton's jigsaw puzzle design with a tapered block characteristic

161
00:14:02,101 --> 00:14:08,600
of a Roman arch. Like Eddystone before it, this structure uses compression to become

162
00:14:08,601 --> 00:14:10,145
stronger as waves hit.

163
00:14:10,146 --> 00:14:14,640
Alexander prepared the site by removing the old iron stumps from

164
00:14:14,641 --> 00:14:20,430
the previous lighthouse and constructing a new iron framework for the ledge.

165
00:14:20,431 --> 00:14:21,131
Work was

166
00:14:21,132 --> 00:14:26,200
difficult as the rocks were often just under water and waves frequently swept men into

167
00:14:26,201 --> 00:14:26,901
the sea.

168
00:14:26,902 --> 00:14:31,740
Concrete blocks fashioned by stone cutters on shore were then hauled out to the

169
00:14:31,741 --> 00:14:37,420
ledge and built up with masonry to create the 89-foot tower. Under such conditions,

170
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:41,866
it took the builders five years to finish the tower.

171
00:14:41,867 --> 00:14:44,840
I'm completely amazed, astounded at the

172
00:14:44,841 --> 00:14:48,980
audacity of the builders of that lighthouse just to think that they could build a structure like

173
00:14:48,981 --> 00:14:54,766
that, a massive granite lighthouse on a ledge that's submerged except at low tide.

174
00:14:54,767 --> 00:14:55,467
I think

175
00:14:55,468 --> 00:14:59,762
it's absolutely phenomenal and something that certainly nobody would try today.

176
00:14:59,763 --> 00:15:00,560
The cost would

177
00:15:00,561 --> 00:15:02,931
phenomenal. The cost was high in those days.

178
00:15:02,932 --> 00:15:05,680
It was one of the most expensive lighthouses built.

179
00:15:06,820 --> 00:15:13,000
With its 22-foot bronze lantern for a hat, the lighthouse topped out at 112 feet,

180
00:15:13,560 --> 00:15:19,560
shining its beacon 15 miles out to sea for the first time on November 15, 1860.

181
00:15:20,820 --> 00:15:26,189
30 feet in diameter at the base and tapering to 14 feet below the lantern.

182
00:15:26,190 --> 00:15:27,640
The lower 40 feet of

183
00:15:27,641 --> 00:15:29,620
the tower was essentially solid rock.

184
00:15:31,060 --> 00:15:34,780
Above were brick-lined rooms for storage, kitchen, and keepers' quarters.

185
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:40,100
Waves occasionally top the lantern room at Minot's Ledge

186
00:15:40,101 --> 00:15:43,660
as blizzards and hurricanes send waves crashing against the tower.

187
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:48,180
But the lighthouse just braces and lets the waves roll off its back,

188
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:51,080
as it has for nearly 150 years.

189
00:15:51,081 --> 00:15:58,080
Locally, people remember Minot's Ledge Lighthouse for a quirk of its beacon.

190
00:16:00,080 --> 00:16:05,520
All lighthouses have a signature of blinking, or not blinking, known as a characteristic.

191
00:16:06,740 --> 00:16:13,460
Since 1894, Minot's Ledge Light has had the most famous characteristic of any lighthouse in America.

192
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:20,800
It's one, one, two, three, four, one, two, three.

193
00:16:21,080 --> 00:16:22,860
One, four, three.

194
00:16:23,080 --> 00:16:25,080
And it keeps repeating that, repeating that.

195
00:16:25,740 --> 00:16:26,920
And it's called the Lover's Light.

196
00:16:27,240 --> 00:16:31,640
The Lover's in on the beach looking out over the water and looking at the lighthouse flashing.

197
00:16:32,040 --> 00:16:38,260
One, I, four, L-O-V-E, three, Y-O-U.

198
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:42,120
So the Coast Guard advertised that they were going to get rid of this.

199
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:43,520
They were going to change this characteristic.

200
00:16:44,300 --> 00:16:46,300
The word got out to the population.

201
00:16:46,301 --> 00:16:51,140
the hue and cry in the Boston papers to the mayor of Boston,

202
00:16:51,260 --> 00:16:54,140
to the Coast Guard app, to their congressmen, to their senators,

203
00:16:54,300 --> 00:16:55,440
was incredible.

204
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,280
You can't change that characteristic.

205
00:16:58,720 --> 00:17:01,060
That's the I love you characteristic.

206
00:17:01,580 --> 00:17:03,420
No, that's the I love you light, they called it.

207
00:17:03,820 --> 00:17:05,480
And people would write in and say,

208
00:17:05,481 --> 00:17:09,060
well, my honey and I, we used to go down to the shore and look out and we'd spark.

209
00:17:09,400 --> 00:17:10,840
Whatever sparking is, you know.

210
00:17:11,540 --> 00:17:13,040
Give her a little kiss on the cheek.

211
00:17:13,041 --> 00:17:20,640
bowing to pressure the coast guard kept the minus ledge characteristic 143

212
00:17:20,641 --> 00:17:25,260
once again making the shores along cohasset safe for lovers

213
00:17:25,261 --> 00:17:32,140
but the i love you story illuminates the single most important aspect of any lighthouse

214
00:17:32,141 --> 00:17:34,240
it's light

215
00:17:34,241 --> 00:17:38,700
next lights and lenses

216
00:17:43,040 --> 00:17:50,040
Light is a revelation in all its aspects along a coast.

217
00:17:50,041 --> 00:17:57,040
The sun is the greatest lighthouse, and daylight, its characteristic.

218
00:17:57,041 --> 00:18:04,040
Between the dawn rays that first tinge the water, to the last light that bathes the skies,

219
00:18:04,041 --> 00:18:11,040
the sun dances on waves, penetrates the seas,

220
00:18:11,041 --> 00:18:13,440
and darts between the clouds.

221
00:18:17,940 --> 00:18:20,680
But nighttime belongs to a lighthouse.

222
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:26,520
The most important feature of any lighthouse is its light,

223
00:18:27,240 --> 00:18:30,920
the beacon that broadcasts its message to ships at sea.

224
00:18:32,340 --> 00:18:35,300
Though early illuminants included wood, coal, and candles,

225
00:18:35,840 --> 00:18:40,080
most of these options had already been abandoned by the early 19th century.

226
00:18:41,040 --> 00:18:46,060
Oil had become the fuel of choice, and wells of oil with solid wicks were used.

227
00:18:46,620 --> 00:18:49,080
But oil was often expensive and sooty.

228
00:18:50,220 --> 00:18:55,580
Whale oil dominated, but when the price skyrocketed in the 1840s, other options were considered.

229
00:18:56,180 --> 00:19:00,080
They tried porpoise oil, olive oil, canola oil.

230
00:19:00,081 --> 00:19:08,320
By the end of the 1850s, they had settled on lard oil, pig lard oil, any kind of lard.

231
00:19:08,321 --> 00:19:10,120
and it came in big white chunks.

232
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:13,760
The keepers had to melt the oil before they put it in the lamp.

233
00:19:14,060 --> 00:19:17,920
And if you were in New England and you had a tall tower and it was February,

234
00:19:18,660 --> 00:19:24,660
you had to really hustle to get up that staircase before that oil congealed and get it into your lamps.

235
00:19:25,900 --> 00:19:27,640
There had to be a better way.

236
00:19:28,280 --> 00:19:29,740
When Swiss scientist M.A.

237
00:19:29,741 --> 00:19:33,360
Argonne accidentally passed a broken beaker over an open fire,

238
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:35,500
he saw the flame shoot up.

239
00:19:35,501 --> 00:19:41,500
Better air flow was created due to the glass chimney he had accidentally discovered.

240
00:19:41,501 --> 00:19:47,500
In the center of Argonne's new lamp was a circular cloth wick, which burned evenly.

241
00:19:47,501 --> 00:19:52,500
Eventually, wicks inside wicks made the process more efficient.

242
00:19:52,501 --> 00:19:56,500
In the early 1900s, another lamp improved things even further.

243
00:19:56,501 --> 00:20:01,500
The IOV is an incandescent oil vapor lamp, IOV.

244
00:20:01,501 --> 00:20:11,600
And it takes kerosene, vaporizes it onto a mantle, a cloth mantle, very much like a Coleman stove you'd use in camping.

245
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:19,740
And it burns very hot, very efficient, and actually held off electricity in many light stations for many years.

246
00:20:20,300 --> 00:20:24,500
But electricity was coming in, and it started to come in after the turn of the century.

247
00:20:24,501 --> 00:20:35,834
Why the IOV held sway for so long is that there weren't electric lines out to these remote light stations, even on the coast, even outside of town sometimes, let alone

248
00:20:35,835 --> 00:20:36,920
island stations.

249
00:20:37,460 --> 00:20:43,520
So it was easier to use the IOV than to string an electric line out to these stations.

250
00:20:45,500 --> 00:20:52,620
Illuminants generate the light in the lighthouse, but a lens is needed to broadcast that light so that it can be seen from great distances.

251
00:20:54,500 --> 00:21:06,500
Glass panels, originally used on Henry Winstanley's Eddystone Lighthouse to protect the fire from the elements, led to experiments with glass reflectors.

252
00:21:06,501 --> 00:21:11,500
People had used glass mirrors to reflect light for thousands of years.

253
00:21:11,501 --> 00:21:15,960
But by the 18th century, lighthouses were adopting the cadoptric system,

254
00:21:16,520 --> 00:21:21,020
in which parabolic reflectors, mirrors molded into parabolas behind lights,

255
00:21:21,620 --> 00:21:26,020
created a single focused beam, even brighter than the original light.

256
00:21:27,100 --> 00:21:29,640
Though the cadoptric system was an improvement,

257
00:21:29,641 --> 00:21:36,560
the dioptric system invented by Augustin Jean Fresnel, a French physicist, was a breakthrough.

258
00:21:36,561 --> 00:21:43,560
Fresnel created an elegant drum of glass that set down around the light source.

259
00:21:43,561 --> 00:21:51,560
If you look at the lens, it has a band, a center band, a magnifying band, called a dioptic.

260
00:21:51,561 --> 00:21:56,560
And that is pure, simple magnification, nothing else.

261
00:21:56,561 --> 00:22:02,560
Then upper and lower, on the upper and lower sides of that, there's what they call catadioptrics,

262
00:22:02,561 --> 00:22:16,561
and they're prisms and they're made into a certain angle and then that prism takes and redirects the light back to form in the lens to form a perfectly solid bar.

263
00:22:19,720 --> 00:22:26,720
Lenses of glass used in spectacles as early as the 15th century were originally employed to magnify images.

264
00:22:26,721 --> 00:22:34,440
Fresnel lenses magnify, but also bend and focus a specific beam of light that is passed

265
00:22:34,441 --> 00:22:36,940
through them.

266
00:22:36,941 --> 00:22:41,660
Fresnel lenses for lighthouses come in several different sizes, from massive first order

267
00:22:41,661 --> 00:22:51,080
lenses, 36.2 inches in radius, to modest 5.9 inch radius, sixth order lenses, plus a middle

268
00:22:51,081 --> 00:22:55,492
size three and a half order lens at 14.7 inches radius.

269
00:22:55,493 --> 00:22:58,220
The most common lighthouse lens is

270
00:22:58,221 --> 00:22:59,576
the fourth order.

271
00:22:59,577 --> 00:23:05,480
First order lenses can broadcast light over 20 miles, but their ability to

272
00:23:05,481 --> 00:23:11,609
throw light depends on more than size. You can only see so far to the horizon.

273
00:23:11,610 --> 00:23:12,310
So the

274
00:23:12,311 --> 00:23:17,140
higher the lighthouse was, the further it could be seen because it would extend over

275
00:23:17,141 --> 00:23:18,600
the horizon

276
00:23:18,601 --> 00:23:19,900
every lighthouse

277
00:23:19,901 --> 00:23:22,920
would have the the distance it could be seen

278
00:23:22,921 --> 00:23:24,140
and this would determine

279
00:23:24,141 --> 00:23:26,260
by the height of the uh...

280
00:23:26,261 --> 00:23:29,140
the uh... the lighthouse the light

281
00:23:29,141 --> 00:23:32,820
by the height of the observer we can't forget him

282
00:23:32,821 --> 00:23:36,760
and i think you've served in fifteen feet

283
00:23:36,761 --> 00:23:39,820
uh... that would be equivalent

284
00:23:39,821 --> 00:23:42,180
x miles

285
00:23:42,181 --> 00:23:43,480
this would be seen

286
00:23:43,481 --> 00:23:44,960
and the sailor could

287
00:23:44,961 --> 00:23:50,680
when that light first popped over the horizon, get an indication of where he might be.

288
00:23:52,980 --> 00:23:57,020
Fresnel lenses also helped create new characteristics for lighthouses.

289
00:23:58,580 --> 00:24:03,740
Bullseyes built into the dioptric portion of the Fresnel lens changed the light from a fixed beam

290
00:24:03,741 --> 00:24:07,600
into a light that regularly focused into a pencil beam when rotated.

291
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:11,800
The light could be constant, blink on and off,

292
00:24:11,801 --> 00:24:15,740
get brighter, or even change color.

293
00:24:17,620 --> 00:24:21,340
Fresnel lenses increase the luminous intensity or candle power of light

294
00:24:21,341 --> 00:24:26,300
and provide lighthouses with signature characteristics that aid identification.

295
00:24:28,280 --> 00:24:32,780
Mariners have a visual guide to tell them either where they are or where a danger is.

296
00:24:33,760 --> 00:24:38,440
A characteristic can tell them which lighthouse they are looking at and what its purpose is.

297
00:24:38,441 --> 00:24:43,440
Since a lighthouse is usually stationed within sight of its nearest lighthouse neighbor,

298
00:24:43,441 --> 00:24:47,440
a knowledgeable mariner can use a technique called triangulation

299
00:24:47,441 --> 00:24:53,440
to determine his location based on a triangle formed by the two lighthouses and his position.

300
00:24:58,440 --> 00:25:04,440
Fresnel lenses bending and broadcasting light, turning on their clockwork rotators,

301
00:25:04,441 --> 00:25:07,440
are the very soul of a lighthouse.

302
00:25:08,440 --> 00:25:15,440
Mesmerizing in their crystal beauty.

303
00:25:19,340 --> 00:25:23,540
But lighthouses have never been only about broadcasting light.

304
00:25:23,541 --> 00:25:27,840
A lighthouse, after all, has to work in the densest fog.

305
00:25:27,841 --> 00:25:29,340
How? Through sound.

306
00:25:29,341 --> 00:25:33,140
Some lighthouse keepers were originally required to fire cannons

307
00:25:33,141 --> 00:25:36,440
to alert ships to their presence in pea soup.

308
00:25:36,441 --> 00:25:40,980
Lighthouse keepers were also required to ring bells, some so large they weighed a ton.

309
00:25:41,780 --> 00:25:46,220
At the turn of the last century, compressed air fog signals,

310
00:25:46,360 --> 00:25:50,060
which could be heard up to eight nautical miles away, replaced the old bells.

311
00:25:51,360 --> 00:25:57,180
Bells, foghorns, and beacons are the basic elements of the familiar Seacliff Lighthouse Tower.

312
00:25:57,820 --> 00:26:01,280
But these elements can be found on lighthouses of all shapes and sizes,

313
00:26:01,800 --> 00:26:03,880
including lighthouses that actually float.

314
00:26:04,460 --> 00:26:06,240
Up next, lightships.

315
00:26:06,440 --> 00:26:10,540
Fernell lenses are not exclusive to lighthouses.

316
00:26:11,480 --> 00:26:17,040
Automobile headlights made of plastic rather than glass are also amplified by the Fernell design.

317
00:26:17,900 --> 00:26:20,280
Lighthouses will return on Modern Marvels.

318
00:26:22,320 --> 00:26:27,260
The image of the lighthouse as a proud tower anchored on a bluff by the sea

319
00:26:27,261 --> 00:26:33,820
is so ingrained in our consciousness that to entertain any other concept is almost unimaginable.

320
00:26:36,440 --> 00:26:41,780
But floating lighthouses guarded our shores as lightships for over a century.

321
00:26:42,760 --> 00:26:46,980
Lightships were really put in places where you couldn't practically build a lighthouse.

322
00:26:47,760 --> 00:26:52,780
Also, the ships can navigate by a lightship without worrying about running aground often.

323
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,860
Where a lighthouse certainly is built right on a piece of rock.

324
00:26:57,600 --> 00:27:03,640
A lightship is, in many times, a point of navigation, a place to aim for.

325
00:27:03,641 --> 00:27:14,640
Lightships began in 18th century England and involved hanging a lantern or other light source to the top of the mast, with a ship anchored to a single spot.

326
00:27:14,641 --> 00:27:22,640
Giant mushroom-shaped anchors were employed, but lightships still managed to get pulled off their station during fierce storms.

327
00:27:22,641 --> 00:27:23,341
storms.

328
00:27:23,342 --> 00:27:30,640
And some got knocked off, lost their anchors, and they were dragged as far as 800

329
00:27:30,641 --> 00:27:36,433
miles off station. But for the most part, those anchors held them pretty solidly.

330
00:27:36,434 --> 00:27:37,220
By the 19th

331
00:27:37,221 --> 00:27:42,260
century, the concept was put to good use in America, particularly off the coast of Cape Cod.

332
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:47,880
Cape Cod was a very treacherous area. There were a lot of shifting sand shoals.

333
00:27:47,881 --> 00:27:56,000
from one moment to the next you didn't know whether a ship could get over a certain sandbar or not

334
00:27:56,001 --> 00:28:03,040
and so there had to be a lot of light ships to guide the ships from New York City and around Cape Cod

335
00:28:03,041 --> 00:28:04,980
into Boston and points north.

336
00:28:05,800 --> 00:28:10,420
So they had the most light ships of any location in the entire country.

337
00:28:10,421 --> 00:28:16,360
The average lightship had a crew of 16 people and one officer.

338
00:28:17,040 --> 00:28:19,080
A chief warrant officer usually was the captain.

339
00:28:20,120 --> 00:28:26,040
And one third of that crew would be ashore at all times.

340
00:28:27,040 --> 00:28:29,040
Lightship duty was extremely difficult.

341
00:28:30,320 --> 00:28:36,020
There would have to be a man out on a deck ringing, tolling a fog signal,

342
00:28:36,021 --> 00:28:41,280
notifying any possible passing vessel

343
00:28:41,281 --> 00:28:44,700
that they were near a shoal or they were,

344
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:46,600
this was a point of navigation.

345
00:28:47,320 --> 00:28:49,060
And this could be at 2 o'clock in the morning,

346
00:28:49,140 --> 00:28:50,980
3 o'clock in the morning, middle of January,

347
00:28:51,520 --> 00:28:55,780
and they'd have to pound this fog signal

348
00:28:55,781 --> 00:28:58,580
once every 10 seconds, all night long.

349
00:28:58,800 --> 00:29:02,580
So you can imagine what kind of duty that was

350
00:29:02,581 --> 00:29:07,600
at 20 degrees below zero with a gale blowing outside,

351
00:29:08,040 --> 00:29:12,020
and you're just standing there up on an open deck pounding away on a fog belt.

352
00:29:13,700 --> 00:29:16,840
Because of their inaccessible location off the coast,

353
00:29:17,200 --> 00:29:20,400
lightships perform their important function in virtual anonymity.

354
00:29:20,940 --> 00:29:23,420
Almost as ignored as the poor lightships,

355
00:29:24,140 --> 00:29:26,400
lighthouses that work large lakes and rivers

356
00:29:26,401 --> 00:29:29,720
also challenge the traditional concept of the seaside lighthouse.

357
00:29:29,721 --> 00:29:34,420
In fact, the Great Lakes are home to literally hundreds of lighthouses.

358
00:29:34,940 --> 00:29:48,940
In the 1850s, 60s, and 70s, there was a flurry of shipping trade, more ships going between Buffalo, Cleveland, Detroit, and that's when the Great Lakes lighthouses

359
00:29:49,433 --> 00:29:50,500
were needed.

360
00:29:51,280 --> 00:29:58,960
Hundreds of short, squat lighthouses popped up along the Great Lakes, not to warn ships of dangerous rocks, but to guide them to port.

361
00:29:58,961 --> 00:30:08,180
In the Great Lakes, the focal plane of most of those lights is about 16 to 18 miles out into the lake,

362
00:30:08,360 --> 00:30:13,960
and it really didn't need to be as tall as the East Coast lighthouses.

363
00:30:15,720 --> 00:30:20,680
Loretta Brandon grew up in one of those lighthouses at Presque Isle in Erie, Pennsylvania,

364
00:30:21,080 --> 00:30:23,440
on a peninsula surrounded by woods and lake.

365
00:30:23,441 --> 00:30:26,940
The lighthouse was very secluded, and I was an only child.

366
00:30:26,941 --> 00:30:35,900
So as a result, I turned to my pets for friendship and for companionship.

367
00:30:36,660 --> 00:30:39,820
I made friends with some of the animals that came to the door.

368
00:30:40,200 --> 00:30:44,320
We had a pet deer. We had a pet snake. We had a pet raccoon.

369
00:30:45,120 --> 00:30:48,820
In the afternoon, my father would come home from work about 2.30 in the afternoon,

370
00:30:49,000 --> 00:30:53,000
and he would go into the den and lay down on the daybed for a nap,

371
00:30:53,001 --> 00:30:58,600
and the deer would curl around his head on the pillow just to be close to him while he napped.

372
00:30:58,680 --> 00:31:00,620
And the deer would also take an afternoon nap.

373
00:31:01,420 --> 00:31:07,480
If lightships and squat freshwater lighthouses clash with the traditional imagery of the proud seaside tower,

374
00:31:08,220 --> 00:31:11,420
the reality of erosion has literally undermined it.

375
00:31:12,280 --> 00:31:17,120
Lighthouses have been moved, rebuilt, and replaced due to the ever-shifting tides.

376
00:31:17,121 --> 00:31:23,180
In fact, the survival of Cape Hatteras Lighthouse in North Carolina was a literal cliffhanger.

377
00:31:24,320 --> 00:31:33,340
The story goes that Alexander Hamilton was only 17 years old when he was passing by in a ship, the Thunderbolt.

378
00:31:34,120 --> 00:31:40,720
And the ship came upon hard times and caught on fire and he almost sank.

379
00:31:40,721 --> 00:31:48,717
As Secretary of the Treasury, Hamilton appropriated funds for a lighthouse at Hatteras, which became known as Mr.

380
00:31:48,718 --> 00:31:49,920
Hamilton's Light.

381
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:56,520
Unfortunately, the station was built on a barrier reef that has continually eroded.

382
00:31:56,521 --> 00:32:10,380
Starting in the 1930s, sandbags and groins, which are low-profile walls built perpendicular to the shore, intended to build up sand along the beach, were installed.

383
00:32:10,381 --> 00:32:15,380
However, these were short-term protection measures.

384
00:32:15,381 --> 00:32:28,380
And the 1,600 feet of beach that that light once enjoyed as a buffer from the high tide dwindled to 120 feet in 1997.

385
00:32:28,381 --> 00:32:32,380
The only way to save the lighthouse was to move it.

386
00:32:32,381 --> 00:32:40,500
In 1998, Congress voted $12 million to pay for the relocation 2,900 feet southwest of the original site.

387
00:32:42,060 --> 00:32:44,560
Excavation began around the base of the lighthouse.

388
00:32:45,920 --> 00:32:50,060
The foundation was removed and weight shifted to temporary support.

389
00:32:50,680 --> 00:32:57,280
As they got more stone out, they kept supporting the lighthouse with more and more of the shoring towers

390
00:32:57,281 --> 00:33:02,940
until the lighthouse was sitting completely on these shoring towers, which had hydraulic jacks in them.

391
00:33:03,600 --> 00:33:08,920
The entire lighthouse tower was then hydraulically jacked up out of its old foundation

392
00:33:08,921 --> 00:33:11,660
to begin the process of its long move.

393
00:33:12,580 --> 00:33:16,600
The engineering team was able to support the lighthouse on roll jacks,

394
00:33:16,601 --> 00:33:20,180
which sent it inching along at a rate of over 100 feet a day.

395
00:33:21,060 --> 00:33:27,360
23 days later, the lighthouse was placed on a brand new foundation by International Jiminy Corporation.

396
00:33:28,160 --> 00:33:33,560
It might have been easier to tear the lighthouse down, but that was not an option for its saviors.

397
00:33:33,561 --> 00:33:39,060
This lighthouse was not born with its history. It has grown its history.

398
00:33:39,740 --> 00:33:47,080
It has seen hundreds of storms, thousands of shipwrecks, two world wars, prowling U-boats,

399
00:33:47,081 --> 00:33:51,600
transfer to the United States Coast Guard, transfer to the Park Service.

400
00:33:53,080 --> 00:34:01,760
It is the icon of this nation's rich maritime heritage.

401
00:34:02,240 --> 00:34:09,760
And its historic legacy continues as the American people made a commitment to preserve their past.

402
00:34:10,480 --> 00:34:15,440
And that historic legacy is also continuing as a window to the past

403
00:34:15,441 --> 00:34:17,400
or which future generations can look.

404
00:34:21,260 --> 00:34:24,180
When President Roosevelt ordered the Coast Guard

405
00:34:24,181 --> 00:34:27,740
to take over jurisdiction of the lighthouse service in 1939,

406
00:34:28,520 --> 00:34:31,880
the process of automating lighthouses was already underway.

407
00:34:32,800 --> 00:34:38,280
In 1910, a Swedish scientist invented a sun relay.

408
00:34:39,080 --> 00:34:42,420
It was an apparatus that, using different types of metal,

409
00:34:42,421 --> 00:34:47,840
could open and close a valve and allow settling to flow

410
00:34:47,841 --> 00:34:51,740
so that during the daytime the light was off

411
00:34:51,741 --> 00:34:55,680
and during the evening or very dark and clement weather the light came on.

412
00:34:56,440 --> 00:35:01,040
This allowed, even back in the teens of the last century,

413
00:35:01,840 --> 00:35:06,140
the lighthouse service to automate less important lighthouses.

414
00:35:07,080 --> 00:35:10,420
Now we have what we call a fog detector.

415
00:35:10,421 --> 00:35:14,940
And this is an electronic device that spelled the death knell of the lighthouse keeper.

416
00:35:15,720 --> 00:35:24,000
Because there was no way for the lighthouse to determine when the fog came in, except to have a man.

417
00:35:25,340 --> 00:35:29,920
Solar panels were installed in some lighthouses, where they collected energy from the sun

418
00:35:29,921 --> 00:35:33,740
and stored it in batteries that supplied the lighthouse with power at night.

419
00:35:34,940 --> 00:35:39,500
Clearly, lighthouse keepers were expensive, obsolete, and expendable.

420
00:35:40,420 --> 00:35:44,280
It was just a matter of time before all the lighthouses in America would be automated,

421
00:35:44,940 --> 00:35:47,240
and lighthouse keepers would be out of a job.

422
00:35:48,660 --> 00:35:54,200
Even the primary function of the lighthouses themselves was being usurped by better, more efficient technology.

423
00:35:57,280 --> 00:36:01,000
Radio transmissions provided two-way ship-to-shore communication.

424
00:36:01,920 --> 00:36:06,380
Radar and later global positioning systems would use satellite technology

425
00:36:06,381 --> 00:36:10,520
to allow even the smallest chips to locate themselves anywhere in the world.

426
00:36:12,160 --> 00:36:17,160
Loran devices broadcast distress signals, again using satellite technology,

427
00:36:17,620 --> 00:36:20,580
so that rescue crews can pick up their signals in minutes.

428
00:36:23,620 --> 00:36:29,400
Even the humble buoys, floating markers that need minimal maintenance and now automated,

429
00:36:29,980 --> 00:36:32,520
were much cheaper than the grand but rickety towers.

430
00:36:32,521 --> 00:36:39,520
Lighthouses seemed old-fashioned, low-tech, and in some cases unnecessary.

431
00:36:39,521 --> 00:36:46,520
The Coast Guard couldn't afford to maintain the lighthouses it didn't need and began shutting some down.

432
00:36:46,521 --> 00:36:53,520
Ironically, as lighthouse keepers and their families started disappearing,

433
00:36:53,521 --> 00:36:57,520
some people began to acknowledge their lonely task.

434
00:36:57,521 --> 00:36:58,315
only task.

435
00:36:58,316 --> 00:37:04,520
In the 1930s, an airplane pilot who had gratefully used a lighthouse to orient

436
00:37:05,120 --> 00:37:10,120
himself in a fog began delivering Christmas bundles to remote lighthouse keepers and their

437
00:37:10,121 --> 00:37:12,455
families as a gesture of thanks.

438
00:37:12,456 --> 00:37:16,540
Edward Rose Snow, an historian and lighthouse lover from

439
00:37:16,541 --> 00:37:22,420
New England, took over the job of flying Santa in the late 1930s and continued delivering

440
00:37:22,421 --> 00:37:26,034
packages for over 40 years from Maine to California.

441
00:37:26,035 --> 00:37:29,300
My mother went with him every year. I went with

442
00:37:29,301 --> 00:37:32,785
him every year from the time I was born until I got married.

443
00:37:32,786 --> 00:37:34,760
And then my husband went one year,

444
00:37:35,040 --> 00:37:44,500
and that was sufficient. But it was just a wonderful thing to do. A few weeks before

445
00:37:44,501 --> 00:37:48,236
Christmas, the seller of the house would become Santa's workshop.

446
00:37:48,237 --> 00:37:49,960
We'd clear the ping pong table

447
00:37:49,961 --> 00:37:51,940
off. We had brown wrapping

448
00:37:51,941 --> 00:37:53,900
paper, we had string, we

449
00:37:53,901 --> 00:37:56,420
had cigars

450
00:37:56,421 --> 00:37:58,180
and cigarettes when they were okay for you,

451
00:37:58,560 --> 00:37:59,600
pen and pencil sets,

452
00:38:00,280 --> 00:38:02,040
balloons, razor

453
00:38:02,041 --> 00:38:03,500
blades, candy,

454
00:38:04,180 --> 00:38:06,240
pocket edition books, and

455
00:38:06,241 --> 00:38:08,160
always there was a copy of my dad's

456
00:38:08,161 --> 00:38:10,100
latest book with a card inside that

457
00:38:10,101 --> 00:38:12,160
said, we have received your package, so that he would know

458
00:38:12,161 --> 00:38:13,340
how successful he was

459
00:38:13,341 --> 00:38:14,620
with the drops.

460
00:38:15,740 --> 00:38:17,660
Despite visits from Santa Claus,

461
00:38:18,000 --> 00:38:19,840
the elimination of lighthouse keepers

462
00:38:19,841 --> 00:38:21,254
continued unabated.

463
00:38:21,255 --> 00:38:26,240
In 1989, the Coast Guard planned to automate Boston Light, the last

464
00:38:26,241 --> 00:38:32,100
manned lighthouse left in America. After a public groundswell of protests, it became

465
00:38:32,101 --> 00:38:36,541
instead the only lighthouse in America that has not been automated.

466
00:38:36,542 --> 00:38:37,800
Rick Himmelrich and

467
00:38:37,801 --> 00:38:42,240
his colleagues survive as a tribute to all the lighthouse keepers who came before them.

468
00:38:42,960 --> 00:38:45,150
I'm the only one left officially.

469
00:38:45,151 --> 00:38:48,800
There are a lot of people that are caretakers and stuff

470
00:38:48,801 --> 00:38:53,560
that the Coast Guard, they've got this program where we turn lights over to private organizations.

471
00:38:53,880 --> 00:38:55,840
Real good program. It's win-win.

472
00:38:56,540 --> 00:39:01,820
Because you've got these, you know, some people refer to them as America's castles, okay, the lighthouses.

473
00:39:02,660 --> 00:39:06,020
And so you've got these private organizations that want to step up to the plate.

474
00:39:07,080 --> 00:39:14,780
And it's a great value for the taxpayers because now the Coast Guard, which, you know, a government organization,

475
00:39:14,781 --> 00:39:16,680
we don't have to maintain the buildings.

476
00:39:16,681 --> 00:39:18,280
We just maintain the optic.

477
00:39:19,320 --> 00:39:22,440
Some retired lighthouses have found a second life.

478
00:39:23,300 --> 00:39:29,820
They've been cleaned up, painted, and put to new uses by local citizens and saviors, like Walter Fanning.

479
00:39:30,520 --> 00:39:35,600
My grandfather was transferred from Yerba Buena Island to the lighthouse on East Brother Island.

480
00:39:36,360 --> 00:39:38,520
And we visited there frequently.

481
00:39:40,020 --> 00:39:43,780
He was a keeper there from 1914 to 1921.

482
00:39:43,781 --> 00:39:48,500
and in 1980 a group proposed to restore East Brother Island,

483
00:39:48,660 --> 00:39:50,940
which had been my grandfather's lighthouse.

484
00:39:51,320 --> 00:39:55,960
So I joined that group and we worked there in the restoration

485
00:39:55,961 --> 00:40:00,800
from February 1980 until October 1980 when the work was completed

486
00:40:00,801 --> 00:40:04,040
and the lighthouse became a bed and breakfast den.

487
00:40:07,360 --> 00:40:09,960
Charlotte Johnson is a lighthouse advocate too.

488
00:40:09,961 --> 00:40:15,220
She helped spearhead an effort to save the Rose Island Lighthouse in Newport, Rhode Island.

489
00:40:16,280 --> 00:40:24,920
The Rose Island Lighthouse operated for 100 years from January 20th, 1870, and it was lit for 100 years.

490
00:40:26,740 --> 00:40:31,660
It was in 1970 when it was taken off the light lists.

491
00:40:32,760 --> 00:40:38,960
It was about 1983, 84 when the federal government declared its surplus a second time,

492
00:40:38,961 --> 00:40:47,680
And that's when the Rose Island Lighthouse Foundation was formed, a group of citizens who wanted to see this lighthouse brought back to its former glory.

493
00:40:48,020 --> 00:40:51,280
We formed a non-profit group and raised the funds independently.

494
00:40:51,920 --> 00:40:58,020
And we ended up getting a whole series of permits for the restoration of the building.

495
00:40:58,580 --> 00:41:02,920
And it was officially opened and relit in 1993.

496
00:41:02,921 --> 00:41:07,920
So we're very, very proud of this accomplishment.

497
00:41:07,921 --> 00:41:14,920
And it's not just a historic building, it's also an environmental education center here.

498
00:41:16,920 --> 00:41:21,920
Matt Marcinek of the Department of Conservation and Natural Resources at Presque Isle State Park,

499
00:41:21,921 --> 00:41:25,920
who now lives and works in the lighthouse, is another lighthouse custodian.

500
00:41:25,921 --> 00:41:31,920
We acquired it from the federal government as excess historic property.

501
00:41:31,921 --> 00:41:40,340
So what our role will be is working with historians, our State Historical and Museum Commission, National Park Service.

502
00:41:40,341 --> 00:41:48,660
We'll continue with the restoration efforts of the lighthouse so that the historical integrity can remain.

503
00:41:49,620 --> 00:41:56,500
Lighthouses have not become merely anachronistic curiosities of interest only to historic preservationists.

504
00:41:57,620 --> 00:42:01,360
Unlike lighthouse keepers, most of them continue their work.

505
00:42:01,920 --> 00:42:12,420
Nowadays we have excellent charts, there's GPS, there's LORAN, but the boaters and the ship captains need a little bit more.

506
00:42:12,600 --> 00:42:19,560
So we have aids to navigation such as lighthouses, lighted buoys, foghorns, radio signals.

507
00:42:20,960 --> 00:42:27,320
So a good ship captain will never rely on one single source for navigation.

508
00:42:28,720 --> 00:42:31,680
There's nothing wrong with redundancy. I mean, systems can fail.

509
00:42:31,920 --> 00:42:36,220
I think a lot of people would miss that light.

510
00:42:36,320 --> 00:42:41,940
A lot of navigators, a lot of mariners out there would be pretty unhappy if we took away their lighthouses.

511
00:42:45,460 --> 00:42:49,760
Lighthouses have finally relinquished their role as primary aids to navigation.

512
00:42:52,360 --> 00:42:57,160
In doing so, they have shown us that they have always fundamentally meant more.

513
00:42:57,161 --> 00:43:02,700
having survived harsh weather and dark skies

514
00:43:02,701 --> 00:43:05,580
crushing waves and erosion

515
00:43:05,581 --> 00:43:13,180
even advancing technology and obsolescence

516
00:43:13,181 --> 00:43:20,620
lighthouses continue to blink their message of hope

517
00:43:20,621 --> 00:43:21,640
to mariners

518
00:43:21,641 --> 00:43:23,200
artists

519
00:43:23,201 --> 00:43:24,380
lovers

520
00:43:24,381 --> 00:43:27,240
wanderers of all stripes

521
00:43:27,241 --> 00:43:31,080
who occasionally find themselves lost in a chaotic world

522
00:43:31,081 --> 00:43:35,380
something that's solid

523
00:43:35,381 --> 00:43:37,820
something that is out there to help you

524
00:43:37,821 --> 00:43:41,200
that is going to be there under the worst conditions

525
00:43:41,201 --> 00:43:42,340
they work the best

526
00:43:42,341 --> 00:43:46,340
and that's their job

527
00:43:46,341 --> 00:43:49,480
that's why they were put on this earth

528
00:43:49,481 --> 00:43:52,880
and there's something real nice about that

529
00:43:52,881 --> 00:43:55,800
especially now when things are moving pretty fast,

530
00:43:56,220 --> 00:44:00,020
to reflect on something that was solid, couldn't be moved,

531
00:44:00,460 --> 00:44:04,800
and was out there to protect people and their lives.

532
00:44:07,300 --> 00:44:10,880
After sending us messages of hope for countless centuries,

533
00:44:11,600 --> 00:44:15,200
we have the opportunity to send a message back to our lighthouses.

534
00:44:15,201 --> 00:44:22,560
And that message is one, four, three.

